# ============================ JLib ============================
# Everything the build produces lives in exactly one place, so the ignore list is short.
# See BUILD.md: OutDir/IntDir are set once in JLib.Common.props and point at build\.

# All build output, for every project and every configuration.
/build/

# Visual Studio's per-user solution state (IntelliSense database, window layout, breakpoints).
.vs/

# Per-user project settings: local debugger arguments, working directory, which machine you
# deployed to. Never the same for two people, and never worth a merge conflict.
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates

# Stray per-project output, in case a project is ever built outside the umbrella (a bare
# msbuild on a .vcxproj with no SolutionDir does exactly this - see the BUILD GOTCHA in BUILD.md
# about $(SolutionDir) being empty, which lands output somewhere unexpected).
x64/
Win32/
[Aa][Rr][Mm]64/

# MSBuild incremental-build bookkeeping and linker leftovers.
*.tlog
*.ilk
*.exp
*.idb
*.iobj
*.ipdb
*.pdb
*.log
*.lastbuildstate
*.recipe

# NuGet, if a project ever reintroduces it. (The DirectX headers came from a package originally;
# they are vendored under ThirdParty/ now precisely so no restore step is needed.)
packages/
*.nupkg

# OS / editor noise.
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/


# ==================== DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED ====================
#
# There is no `*.lib` rule, and no bare `Debug/` or `Release/` rule. That is not an oversight:
#
#   ThirdParty/DirectXTex/lib/debug/DirectXTex.lib
#   ThirdParty/DirectXTex/lib/release/DirectXTex.lib
#
# are VENDORED binaries that must be committed - DirectXTex has no local source tree, so a fresh
# clone cannot link without them. A `Release/` rule would swallow that directory (git's ignore
# matching is case-insensitive on Windows, so it catches `release/` too), and a negation such as
# `!ThirdParty/**` CANNOT rescue it: git will not re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded. Build output is caught by the `/build/` rule above instead, which is precise and has
# no such collision.
#
# If you ever add a `*.lib` or `Release/` rule, run this first and expect two paths back:
#   git check-ignore -v ThirdParty/DirectXTex/lib/release/DirectXTex.lib
# Silence means it is about to be dropped from the repo.
